1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,832
Chapter one: linear attention.

2
00:00:02,152 --> 00:00:03,826
Here is the problem it solves.

3
00:00:04,146 --> 00:00:08,834
Softmax attention keeps a record for every past token, a key and a value.

4
00:00:09,154 --> 00:00:12,861
To answer a new query, it scores the query against every key.

5
00:00:13,181 --> 00:00:16,293
The weights have to be normalized over all past tokens,

6
00:00:16,293 --> 00:00:17,990
so nothing can be thrown away.

7
00:00:18,310 --> 00:00:20,577
The cache grows with every token you read.

8
00:00:21,897 --> 00:00:23,908
Linear attention makes one change.

9
00:00:24,228 --> 00:00:25,536
It drops the softmax.

10
00:00:25,856 --> 00:00:30,207
Then the weight on value j is just the score, k j transpose q t.

11
00:00:30,497 --> 00:00:32,214
Now the products can be regrouped.

12
00:00:32,534 --> 00:00:34,084
Pull the query out of the sum.

13
00:00:34,404 --> 00:00:38,648
What is left in the bracket, the sum of v j times k j transpose,

14
00:00:38,648 --> 00:00:41,035
does not depend on the query at all.

15
00:00:41,355 --> 00:00:42,765
Call it the state, S t.

16
00:00:43,085 --> 00:00:45,401
The output is just S t times q t.

17
00:00:47,497 --> 00:00:50,659
So the state can be kept up to date, one token at a time.

18
00:00:50,979 --> 00:00:52,286
Step one is the write.

19
00:00:52,606 --> 00:00:57,464
When token t arrives, take the outer product of its value and its key,

20
00:00:57,464 --> 00:00:59,129
v t times k t transpose.

21
00:00:59,449 --> 00:01:02,545
That is a whole matrix, and it is added onto the state.

22
00:01:02,865 --> 00:01:04,166
Step two is the read.

23
00:01:04,486 --> 00:01:10,494
Multiply the state by the query, and out comes the output. o t equals S t times q t.

24
00:01:10,814 --> 00:01:12,206
Step three: discard.

25
00:01:12,526 --> 00:01:14,955
The key and the value are not needed any more.

26
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:16,885
They live inside the state now.

27
00:01:17,205 --> 00:01:19,013
The next token does the same thing.

28
00:01:19,333 --> 00:01:20,645
Write, read, discard.

29
00:01:21,965 --> 00:01:23,253
How big is this state?

30
00:01:23,573 --> 00:01:30,891
With a head width of 128, it is 128 by 128, which is 16,384 numbers,

31
00:01:30,891 --> 00:01:33,043
whatever the length.

32
00:01:33,363 --> 00:01:37,994
A KV cache holds 2 times N times 128 numbers per head.

33
00:01:38,314 --> 00:01:41,973
So the cache passes the state after only 64 tokens.

34
00:01:42,293 --> 00:01:47,750
At 128 thousand tokens, the cache is 2,048 times larger.

35
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:51,218
And every decode step reads the same fixed matrix.

36
00:01:52,938 --> 00:01:54,631
So what does the state remember?

37
00:01:54,951 --> 00:01:58,978
It is a lookup table from keys to values, stored as one matrix.

38
00:01:59,298 --> 00:02:02,728
To test the lookup, read it with one of the stored keys, k i.

39
00:02:03,048 --> 00:02:07,934
The result is v i, the value we want, plus a sum over every other value,

40
00:02:07,934 --> 00:02:11,124
weighted by how much its key overlaps with k i.

41
00:02:11,444 --> 00:02:13,159
That second part is crosstalk.

42
00:02:14,972 --> 00:02:18,676
If all the keys are orthogonal, every overlap is zero,

43
00:02:18,676 --> 00:02:20,940
and the read returns exactly v i.

44
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:23,125
Picture two keys at right angles.

45
00:02:23,445 --> 00:02:26,637
Read with the first one, and the second contributes nothing.

46
00:02:26,938 --> 00:02:32,279
But a space with d k dimensions has room for only d k orthogonal directions.

47
00:02:32,599 --> 00:02:36,455
Add a third key in two dimensions, and it has to overlap the others.

48
00:02:36,775 --> 00:02:40,689
Now a read with the first key also picks up part of the third value.

49
00:02:41,009 --> 00:02:43,595
Here is the same thing as a grid of overlaps.

50
00:02:43,915 --> 00:02:48,531
With four orthogonal keys in four dimensions, only the diagonal is lit,

51
00:02:48,531 --> 00:02:50,026
so every read is clean.

52
00:02:50,346 --> 00:02:54,439
With eight keys in four dimensions, the off-diagonal cells light up,

53
00:02:54,439 --> 00:02:58,051
and every read mixes in a little of every overlapping value.

54
00:02:58,371 --> 00:03:03,686
So a d v by d k state can hold only about d k key value pairs exactly.

55
00:03:04,006 --> 00:03:06,025
A KV cache holds all N of them.

56
00:03:06,345 --> 00:03:08,812
This one fact drives everything that follows.

57
00:03:09,132 --> 00:03:13,571
The later mechanisms try to keep the table clean, but none of them makes it larger.

58
00:03:15,291 --> 00:03:19,798
Dropping the softmax looks like a small change, but the softmax did two jobs.

59
00:03:20,118 --> 00:03:21,821
The first is sharp focus.

60
00:03:22,141 --> 00:03:24,279
Take two keys with scores 5 and 3.

61
00:03:24,599 --> 00:03:28,567
Softmax gives the first key e to the 5 over e to the 3,

62
00:03:28,567 --> 00:03:31,526
about 7.4 times the weight of the second.

63
00:03:31,846 --> 00:03:36,411
A plain linear score gives only 5 over 3, about 1.7 times.

64
00:03:36,731 --> 00:03:40,066
So with linear scores, many slightly matching keys add up,

65
00:03:40,066 --> 00:03:42,424
and they can drown out the one right key.

66
00:03:43,331 --> 00:03:45,751
The second job: weights that sum to one.

67
00:03:46,071 --> 00:03:47,624
Without the normalization,

68
00:03:47,624 --> 00:03:51,687
the size of the output drifts as more tokens are added to the state,

69
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:53,659
and that makes training unstable.

70
00:03:54,565 --> 00:03:58,380
The first version, the Linear Transformer from 2020,

71
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:03,296
replaced the exponential of q dot k with a dot product of features,

72
00:04:03,296 --> 00:04:04,910
phi of q and phi of k.

73
00:04:05,230 --> 00:04:10,996
It used a positive feature map, elu plus one, and kept a normalizer to divide by.

74
00:04:11,316 --> 00:04:15,489
Later versions dropped the normalizer, because it made gradients unstable.

75
00:04:15,809 --> 00:04:18,773
Instead, they normalize q and k to length one,

76
00:04:18,773 --> 00:04:21,607
and add a normalization layer on the output.

77
00:04:22,527 --> 00:04:27,182
Note that this is a different model, not a faster way to compute softmax attention.

78
00:04:27,502 --> 00:04:29,989
A model trained with softmax cannot switch.

79
00:04:30,309 --> 00:04:33,381
It has to be trained with linear attention from the start.

80
00:04:35,101 --> 00:04:37,776
The same layer can be computed in three ways,

81
00:04:37,776 --> 00:04:40,391
and all three give exactly the same outputs.

82
00:04:40,711 --> 00:04:44,992
The parallel form builds the full score matrix, Q K transpose,

83
00:04:44,992 --> 00:04:47,961
applies a causal mask, and multiplies by V.

84
00:04:48,281 --> 00:04:52,071
It is fully parallel, but its cost grows as N squared times d,

85
00:04:52,071 --> 00:04:54,088
which is what we wanted to avoid.

86
00:04:55,008 --> 00:04:58,964
The recurrent form is the token by token loop: write, then read.

87
00:04:59,284 --> 00:05:04,390
Its cost grows only as N times d squared, but every step waits for the one before.

88
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:07,405
That wastes a GPU during training and prefill.

89
00:05:07,725 --> 00:05:09,616
The chunkwise form mixes the two,

90
00:05:09,616 --> 00:05:13,054
and it is the one used in practice for training and prefill.

91
00:05:13,374 --> 00:05:18,743
Cut the sequence into chunks of C tokens, usually 64 to 256.

92
00:05:19,063 --> 00:05:23,226
Inside a chunk, compute the parallel form on the small C by C block.

93
00:05:23,546 --> 00:05:27,604
For all earlier chunks, read the state passed in from the previous chunk,

94
00:05:27,604 --> 00:05:28,215
and add it.

95
00:05:28,535 --> 00:05:30,528
Then update the state, once per chunk.

96
00:05:30,848 --> 00:05:32,881
All three steps are matrix products.

97
00:05:33,201 --> 00:05:37,559
Here are eight outputs from a small random example, computed all three ways.

98
00:05:37,879 --> 00:05:39,040
They match exactly.

99
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:45,474
For one head with d equal to 128, chunks of 64, and 32 thousand tokens,

100
00:05:45,474 --> 00:05:52,707
the chunkwise form costs about 98 thousand FLOPs per token, in 512 sequential steps.

101
00:05:53,027 --> 00:05:57,792
The recurrent loop costs 65 thousand FLOPs per token,

102
00:05:57,792 --> 00:06:00,579
but in 32,768 sequential steps.

103
00:06:00,899 --> 00:06:04,720
The quadratic form costs 8.4 million FLOPs per token.

104
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:08,542
Decode then uses the recurrent loop, one token at a time.

105
00:06:10,262 --> 00:06:11,060
To recap.

106
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:15,933
Linear attention folds the whole history into one fixed matrix per head.

107
00:06:16,253 --> 00:06:18,902
Write with an outer product, read with the query.

108
00:06:19,822 --> 00:06:21,888
Left over: the state only adds.

109
00:06:22,208 --> 00:06:24,496
Nothing is ever forgotten or overwritten.

110
00:06:24,816 --> 00:06:28,837
So after many tokens, the table is full of overlapping entries,

111
00:06:28,837 --> 00:06:31,070
and every read is mostly crosstalk.

112
00:06:31,390 --> 00:06:32,502
Next: forgetting.

113
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:35,115
Chapter two: forgetting.

114
00:06:35,435 --> 00:06:38,122
At the end of chapter one, the state could only add.

115
00:06:38,442 --> 00:06:41,945
After many tokens it is crowded with overlapping entries,

116
00:06:41,945 --> 00:06:44,096
and every read is mostly crosstalk.

117
00:06:44,416 --> 00:06:48,031
The simplest fix is to let the state fade a little before each write.

118
00:06:49,333 --> 00:06:54,280
Multiply the old state by a decay, alpha t, somewhere between zero and one.

119
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:56,934
Then add the new outer product, as before.

120
00:06:58,033 --> 00:06:59,060
Now unroll it.

121
00:06:59,380 --> 00:07:04,566
A token j that was written earlier has been multiplied by every decay since then.

122
00:07:04,886 --> 00:07:08,622
So its weight in the output carries the product of the alphas,

123
00:07:08,622 --> 00:07:09,948
from j plus 1 up to t.

124
00:07:10,268 --> 00:07:14,278
The output is attention without the softmax, with a weight mask, D.

125
00:07:15,367 --> 00:07:17,846
Picture D as a lower triangular grid.

126
00:07:18,166 --> 00:07:22,778
The diagonal is one, and each step away from it multiplies in one more decay.

127
00:07:23,098 --> 00:07:26,818
Plain linear attention is the case where every alpha is one,

128
00:07:26,818 --> 00:07:29,484
so its mask is all ones below the diagonal.

129
00:07:29,804 --> 00:07:32,868
With a decay, the mask fades away from the diagonal.

130
00:07:33,188 --> 00:07:36,540
It also tells the model how far away each token is,

131
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:40,352
so these layers usually need no other positional encoding.

132
00:07:42,067 --> 00:07:47,032
RetNet, from 2023, uses one fixed decay per head, called gamma.

133
00:07:47,352 --> 00:07:52,811
Then D t j is gamma to the power t minus j, and old tokens fade at a set rate.

134
00:07:53,131 --> 00:07:57,264
A head remembers roughly the last one over one minus gamma tokens.

135
00:07:57,584 --> 00:08:03,257
With gamma equal to 0.99, the horizon is about 100 tokens,

136
00:08:03,257 --> 00:08:10,593
and a token 100 steps back keeps 0.99 to the 100, about 0.37 of its weight.

137
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:15,658
Different heads get different decays, so together they cover several scales.

138
00:08:15,978 --> 00:08:22,373
RetNet's experiments space one minus gamma from one over 32 to one over 512.

139
00:08:22,693 --> 00:08:27,084
So the horizons run from about 32 to 512 tokens.

140
00:08:27,404 --> 00:08:33,004
Lightning attention, from 2025, keeps this fixed decay and adds a fast kernel.

141
00:08:33,300 --> 00:08:35,885
The price is that the decay ignores content.

142
00:08:36,205 --> 00:08:40,031
An important number and a greeting fade at exactly the same rate.

143
00:08:41,733 --> 00:08:45,399
The next step is to let each token decide how much to forget.

144
00:08:45,719 --> 00:08:47,327
Mamba-2, from 2024,

145
00:08:47,327 --> 00:08:54,861
uses one decay per head that depends on the token. a t equals e to the delta t times A h.

146
00:08:55,181 --> 00:08:58,798
A h is negative, and delta t is computed from the input.

147
00:08:59,118 --> 00:09:00,944
So the mask is no longer smooth.

148
00:09:01,264 --> 00:09:05,040
A token can choose to keep almost everything, or to forget a lot.

149
00:09:05,933 --> 00:09:12,435
Its paper shows that this state space model is the same computation as the masked linear attention above,

150
00:09:12,435 --> 00:09:14,045
under a simple dictionary.

151
00:09:14,365 --> 00:09:16,806
The SSM input, x, plays the value.

152
00:09:17,126 --> 00:09:18,238
B plays the key.

153
00:09:18,558 --> 00:09:19,928
And C plays the query.

154
00:09:20,248 --> 00:09:22,168
This is called the state space duality.

155
00:09:22,467 --> 00:09:27,384
Because the decay is one scalar per head, the whole layer runs as matrix products,

156
00:09:27,384 --> 00:09:31,462
reported two to eight times faster than the scan of the first Mamba.

157
00:09:31,782 --> 00:09:35,398
One catch: delta t sets both the decay and the write strength.

158
00:09:35,718 --> 00:09:37,823
So a strong write also forgets more.

159
00:09:39,533 --> 00:09:44,182
GLA, gated linear attention, from 2023, goes finer.

160
00:09:44,502 --> 00:09:46,580
It decays each key channel separately.

161
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:50,811
The old state is multiplied on the right by a diagonal matrix,

162
00:09:50,811 --> 00:09:52,766
with one decay per key channel.

163
00:09:53,867 --> 00:09:56,793
Each column of the state belongs to one key channel.

164
00:09:57,113 --> 00:10:01,132
So one channel can be cleared at a topic switch, while the others are kept.

165
00:10:01,433 --> 00:10:05,099
The decays come from a sigmoid, raised to the power one sixteenth.

166
00:10:05,419 --> 00:10:09,488
That power keeps them close to one, so by default the state forgets slowly.

167
00:10:09,808 --> 00:10:22,674
Sigmoid outputs of 0.01, 0.1, 0.5 and 0.9 become decays of 0.75, 0.866,

168
00:10:22,674 --> 00:10:25,574
0.958 and 0.993.

169
00:10:26,667 --> 00:10:29,551
A diagonal decay still trains well in chunks,

170
00:10:29,551 --> 00:10:33,141
because the products of decays become sums in log space.

171
00:10:34,833 --> 00:10:39,319
Mamba-3, from 2026, changes the write in three ways.

172
00:10:39,639 --> 00:10:41,327
First, a trapezoid write.

173
00:10:41,647 --> 00:10:44,766
Each write averages the inputs at both ends of the step.

174
00:10:45,086 --> 00:10:52,021
With a toy step of 0.5 and a decay of 0.8, Mamba-2 writes 0.5 of x t.

175
00:10:52,341 --> 00:10:58,518
The trapezoid rule writes 0.2 of x t minus 1, and 0.25 of x t.

176
00:10:58,833 --> 00:11:00,128
Second, rotations.

177
00:11:00,448 --> 00:11:05,360
A complex valued state turns the real decay into a decay plus a rotation.

178
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:09,016
That works like a position encoding that depends on the data.

179
00:11:09,333 --> 00:11:10,203
Third, MIMO.

180
00:11:10,523 --> 00:11:15,420
Writes of rank 4 instead of rank 1 do about four times the work on the same state.

181
00:11:15,740 --> 00:11:21,113
Decode is limited by bytes, not compute, so the reported latency stays about flat.

182
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:23,598
To recap.

183
00:11:23,918 --> 00:11:27,246
Decay multiplies the old state by alpha before each write.

184
00:11:27,566 --> 00:11:31,897
It is fixed per head in RetNet, chosen by the input in Mamba-2,

185
00:11:31,897 --> 00:11:34,028
and set per key channel in GLA.

186
00:11:34,933 --> 00:11:37,387
Left over: decay fades everything a little.

187
00:11:37,707 --> 00:11:40,988
It cannot remove one wrong association and keep the rest.

188
00:11:41,308 --> 00:11:45,044
And a scalar decay also fades memories that were still correct.

189
00:11:45,364 --> 00:11:48,148
Next: correcting the state, with the delta rule.

190
00:11:49,467 --> 00:11:52,057
Chapter three: correcting, with the delta rule.

191
00:11:52,377 --> 00:11:53,725
Decay can only fade.

192
00:11:54,045 --> 00:11:58,546
If the state holds one wrong association, decay cannot remove just that one.

193
00:11:58,866 --> 00:12:02,322
It fades everything, including memories that were still correct.

194
00:12:02,642 --> 00:12:06,226
The delta rule fixes this by checking the table before it writes.

195
00:12:07,534 --> 00:12:14,592
DeltaNet, from 2021, with a parallel training method in 2024, works in three steps.

196
00:12:14,912 --> 00:12:15,331
First,

197
00:12:15,331 --> 00:12:20,910
read what the state currently returns for the new key: v hat equals S t minus 1,

198
00:12:20,910 --> 00:12:21,607
times k t.

199
00:12:21,927 --> 00:12:23,943
Second, compare it with the new value.

200
00:12:24,263 --> 00:12:26,229
The error is v t minus v hat.

201
00:12:26,549 --> 00:12:30,980
Third, write only the error, scaled by a strength, beta, between zero and one.

202
00:12:31,267 --> 00:12:37,279
Rearranged, the same rule reads: the old state times I minus beta k k transpose,

203
00:12:37,279 --> 00:12:39,082
plus beta v k transpose.

204
00:12:39,402 --> 00:12:44,112
If the state already holds the right value, the error is zero, and nothing changes.

205
00:12:44,432 --> 00:12:47,454
If it holds a stale value, that value is replaced.

206
00:12:49,567 --> 00:12:50,887
Take a toy example.

207
00:12:51,207 --> 00:12:54,027
The state maps two order numbers to a shipping day,

208
00:12:54,027 --> 00:12:55,852
with one key direction per order.

209
00:12:56,172 --> 00:13:00,283
Order 7314 ships on day 2, a Tuesday.

210
00:13:00,603 --> 00:13:03,432
Order 2208 ships on day 4.

211
00:13:03,752 --> 00:13:10,190
Now a new fact arrives: order 7314 now ships on day 5, a Friday.

212
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:14,320
Linear attention just adds 5 on top of 2, and returns 7.

213
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:17,093
That is a mix of the old answer and the new one.

214
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:20,316
DeltaNet reads 2, and finds an error of 3.

215
00:13:20,636 --> 00:13:25,763
With beta equal to 1, it writes 3, and now the state returns exactly 5.

216
00:13:26,083 --> 00:13:30,071
With beta equal to one half, it moves halfway, to 3.5.

217
00:13:30,391 --> 00:13:33,700
Either way, order 2208 stays at 4.

218
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,122
But the edit is clean only along the key that was written.

219
00:13:37,442 --> 00:13:41,280
A query halfway between the two order keys also sees the change.

220
00:13:41,600 --> 00:13:46,044
It reads 4.24 before the update, and 6.36 after.

221
00:13:47,734 --> 00:13:51,797
Now look at the transition itself, I minus beta k k transpose.

222
00:13:52,117 --> 00:13:54,774
It leaves every direction except k untouched.

223
00:13:55,094 --> 00:13:58,102
Along k, it multiplies by one minus beta.

224
00:13:58,422 --> 00:14:02,374
So its eigenvalues are all ones, plus a single one minus beta.

225
00:14:03,267 --> 00:14:05,190
Watch a vector as beta grows.

226
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:09,535
With beta between zero and one, the old entry along k is shrunk.

227
00:14:09,855 --> 00:14:12,664
With beta equal to one, it is erased completely,

228
00:14:12,664 --> 00:14:14,771
and the write puts in the new value.

229
00:14:15,091 --> 00:14:17,414
With beta equal to two, it is flipped.

230
00:14:17,734 --> 00:14:20,870
That is a reflection, and it will matter for state tracking.

231
00:14:22,567 --> 00:14:28,668
Gated DeltaNet, from 2024, combines both tools: decay first, then correct.

232
00:14:28,988 --> 00:14:32,077
The decay, alpha, can clear a whole head at once,

233
00:14:32,077 --> 00:14:34,978
and the delta step edits a single association.

234
00:14:35,298 --> 00:14:39,195
Mamba-2 had only the first tool, and DeltaNet only the second.

235
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:41,133
The order matters.

236
00:14:41,453 --> 00:14:45,061
The state fades first, so the error is read from the faded state.

237
00:14:45,381 --> 00:14:51,903
In the toy, with alpha 0.8 and beta 0.5, the state fades from 2 and 4,

238
00:14:51,903 --> 00:14:53,301
to 1.6 and 3.2.

239
00:14:53,621 --> 00:14:58,430
The read is 1.6, the error is 3.4, and the write adds 1.7.

240
00:14:58,750 --> 00:15:01,094
The result is 3.3 and 3.2.

241
00:15:01,414 --> 00:15:09,065
The price: a scalar decay also fades the unrelated order 2208, from 4 down to 3.2.

242
00:15:09,767 --> 00:15:13,010
KDA, Kimi Delta Attention, from 2025,

243
00:15:13,010 --> 00:15:18,006
puts GLA's per-channel decay inside the gated delta rule.

244
00:15:18,326 --> 00:15:24,974
In the toy, with alpha equal to 0.8 for the first channel and 1 for the second,

245
00:15:24,974 --> 00:15:27,667
only order 7314's channel fades.

246
00:15:27,987 --> 00:15:30,043
The result is 3.3 and 4.

247
00:15:30,363 --> 00:15:32,786
Order 2208 is kept.

248
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:39,390
KDA's speed trick is that both low rank vectors in its transition are tied to k t.

249
00:15:39,710 --> 00:15:46,372
That is reported to make its kernels about two times faster than a general diagonal plus low rank transition.

250
00:15:48,067 --> 00:15:55,382
RWKV-7 and Comba, from 2025, write the same idea as a sum instead of a product.

251
00:15:55,702 --> 00:15:59,332
The decay and the erase are both applied to the old state,

252
00:15:59,332 --> 00:16:01,647
so the erase reads the unfaded value.

253
00:16:01,967 --> 00:16:08,008
In the toy, the erase reads 2 instead of 1.6, and removes 1 instead of 0.8.

254
00:16:08,328 --> 00:16:12,708
The result is 3.1 and 3.2, instead of 3.3 and 3.2.

255
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:18,866
RWKV-7 also makes the decay and the write strength vectors over key channels.

256
00:16:19,186 --> 00:16:21,971
Its removal key differs from its write key,

257
00:16:21,971 --> 00:16:25,922
so its transition is a general diagonal plus low rank matrix.

258
00:16:26,242 --> 00:16:29,001
That costs about twice KDA's work per chunk.

259
00:16:30,900 --> 00:16:34,557
Some tasks need the state to track something, not just remember it.

260
00:16:34,877 --> 00:16:39,342
The simplest is parity: is the number of 1 bits so far even, or odd?

261
00:16:39,662 --> 00:16:47,561
A linear RNN whose transition has only positive eigenvalues provably cannot solve parity in finite precision.

262
00:16:47,881 --> 00:16:50,131
The state can only shrink or stay the same.

263
00:16:50,451 --> 00:16:51,557
It can never flip.

264
00:16:51,867 --> 00:16:56,281
The fix is to let beta go up to 2, for example two times a sigmoid.

265
00:16:56,601 --> 00:17:00,111
Then the eigenvalue, one minus beta, reaches minus 1.

266
00:17:00,431 --> 00:17:01,662
That is a reflection.

267
00:17:01,982 --> 00:17:03,743
On the bits 1 0 1 1 0 1,

268
00:17:03,743 --> 00:17:08,805
a one number state that flips its sign at every 1 bit ends at plus 1,

269
00:17:08,805 --> 00:17:11,153
correctly showing an even count.

270
00:17:12,067 --> 00:17:17,737
DeltaProduct, from 2025, goes further and takes several delta steps per token.

271
00:17:18,057 --> 00:17:20,047
Two reflections make a rotation.

272
00:17:20,367 --> 00:17:24,694
For example, two mirrors 30 degrees apart give a 60 degree rotation,

273
00:17:24,694 --> 00:17:26,476
and the state does not grow.

274
00:17:26,767 --> 00:17:29,482
Note that this adds expressivity, not capacity.

275
00:17:29,802 --> 00:17:32,368
The state can track more kinds of structure,

276
00:17:32,368 --> 00:17:34,759
but it still holds about d k exact pairs.

277
00:17:36,467 --> 00:17:38,132
One more detail: training.

278
00:17:38,452 --> 00:17:42,805
For a decay alone, a chunk only needs cumulative products of decays.

279
00:17:43,125 --> 00:17:45,859
But the delta rule's transitions do not commute.

280
00:17:46,179 --> 00:17:50,941
So the product of a chunk's transitions is rewritten in a compact form,

281
00:17:50,941 --> 00:17:54,495
called the WY or UT form, before the matrix products.

282
00:17:55,800 --> 00:17:56,598
To recap.

283
00:17:56,918 --> 00:18:00,237
The delta rule reads what the state returns for the new key,

284
00:18:00,237 --> 00:18:01,675
and writes only the error.

285
00:18:01,995 --> 00:18:06,916
Gated DeltaNet puts a decay in front, and KDA makes that decay per channel.

286
00:18:07,236 --> 00:18:10,626
Letting beta reach 2 adds reflections, for state tracking.

287
00:18:11,534 --> 00:18:17,205
Left over: the delta rule keeps the table clean, but the table is still d v by d k.

288
00:18:17,525 --> 00:18:19,162
A better write cannot add room.

289
00:18:19,482 --> 00:18:21,835
Next, all of these rules on one axis.

290
00:18:23,133 --> 00:18:24,831
Chapter four: one axis.

291
00:18:25,151 --> 00:18:28,079
We now have three tools: add, fade, and correct.

292
00:18:28,399 --> 00:18:32,580
They look like different ideas, but every rule so far has the same shape.

293
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:36,762
S t equals S t minus 1 times A t, plus W t.

294
00:18:37,866 --> 00:18:39,363
W t is the write.

295
00:18:39,683 --> 00:18:42,401
It is almost always a scaled v k transpose.

296
00:18:42,721 --> 00:18:46,879
A t is the transition: what happens to the old state before the write.

297
00:18:47,199 --> 00:18:49,417
The methods differ almost only in A t.

298
00:18:49,737 --> 00:18:54,086
So let's line them up, and watch what each transition does to the space of keys.

299
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:55,610
One detail helps.

300
00:18:55,930 --> 00:18:59,773
A t multiplies the state from the right, so it acts on the key side.

301
00:19:00,093 --> 00:19:02,831
Each column of S belongs to one key direction.

302
00:19:03,151 --> 00:19:06,330
That is why a diagonal A t fades key channels,

303
00:19:06,330 --> 00:19:10,132
and why I minus beta k k transpose edits along one key.

304
00:19:12,633 --> 00:19:15,622
A t equal to the identity is linear attention.

305
00:19:15,942 --> 00:19:17,622
It does nothing to the old state.

306
00:19:17,942 --> 00:19:18,915
It only adds.

307
00:19:19,235 --> 00:19:21,134
A chunk is trained with plain sums.

308
00:19:21,433 --> 00:19:27,221
A scalar times the identity, gamma or alpha t, covers RetNet, Lightning and Mamba-2.

309
00:19:27,541 --> 00:19:30,429
It fades everything, equally, in every direction.

310
00:19:30,749 --> 00:19:33,799
A chunk needs a cumulative product of the decays.

311
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:37,033
A diagonal matrix of decays is GLA.

312
00:19:37,353 --> 00:19:39,492
It fades each key channel on its own,

313
00:19:39,492 --> 00:19:42,961
so one direction can shrink fast while another barely moves.

314
00:19:43,281 --> 00:19:46,266
A chunk uses a cumulative sum, in log space.

315
00:19:46,566 --> 00:19:49,538
I minus beta k k transpose is DeltaNet.

316
00:19:49,858 --> 00:19:53,673
It overwrites along one direction, k, and leaves the rest alone.

317
00:19:53,993 --> 00:19:58,303
Gated DeltaNet puts a scalar decay in front: fade, then overwrite.

318
00:19:58,623 --> 00:20:03,312
KDA puts a diagonal decay in front: fade per channel, then overwrite.

319
00:20:03,632 --> 00:20:07,156
All three train chunks with the WY or UT form.

320
00:20:07,466 --> 00:20:13,432
RWKV-7 and Comba use the sum form: a diagonal, minus a low rank term.

321
00:20:13,752 --> 00:20:17,859
Their chunks are trained as a general diagonal plus low rank matrix.

322
00:20:18,166 --> 00:20:23,055
And products of reflections, or rotations, as in DeltaProduct and Mamba-3,

323
00:20:23,055 --> 00:20:25,367
can track structure such as parity.

324
00:20:25,687 --> 00:20:28,492
These are trained per sub-step, or as rotations.

325
00:20:29,800 --> 00:20:31,877
Remember the order toy from chapter three.

326
00:20:32,197 --> 00:20:36,199
The same new fact, order 7314 now ships on day 5,

327
00:20:36,199 --> 00:20:40,037
gives a different answer under each transition.

328
00:20:40,357 --> 00:20:41,496
Adding gives 7.

329
00:20:41,816 --> 00:20:44,755
The delta rule gives exactly 5, and keeps 4.

330
00:20:45,075 --> 00:20:51,243
Gated DeltaNet gives 3.3, but fades order 2208 to 3.2.

331
00:20:51,563 --> 00:20:53,889
KDA gives 3.3, and keeps 4.

332
00:20:54,209 --> 00:20:56,811
The sum form gives 3.1 and 3.2.

333
00:20:57,131 --> 00:20:59,050
Same write, different transitions.

334
00:21:00,766 --> 00:21:03,162
Here is why the training column splits in two.

335
00:21:03,482 --> 00:21:05,996
Diagonal transitions commute with each other.

336
00:21:06,316 --> 00:21:09,814
Apply two of them in either order, and you land in the same place.

337
00:21:10,134 --> 00:21:12,793
So a chunk needs only cumulative decays.

338
00:21:13,113 --> 00:21:15,728
Householder-like transitions do not commute.

339
00:21:16,048 --> 00:21:18,328
Swap the order, and the result changes.

340
00:21:18,648 --> 00:21:21,362
So they need the WY or UT rewrite.

341
00:21:23,066 --> 00:21:28,536
The 2026 research models refine the delta rule along this same axis.

342
00:21:28,856 --> 00:21:33,452
They separate how strongly to erase, how strongly to write, and where to erase.

343
00:21:33,772 --> 00:21:36,470
That means per channel erase and write gates,

344
00:21:36,470 --> 00:21:39,288
an erase direction separate from the write key,

345
00:21:39,288 --> 00:21:42,045
or a step size set by an uncertainty estimate.

346
00:21:42,365 --> 00:21:44,804
None of them changes the size of the state.

347
00:21:46,100 --> 00:21:47,802
So think of A t as a dial.

348
00:21:48,122 --> 00:21:49,551
At one end, it does nothing.

349
00:21:49,871 --> 00:21:51,027
Turn it, and it fades.

350
00:21:51,347 --> 00:21:53,441
Turn it further, and it fades per channel.

351
00:21:53,761 --> 00:21:55,541
Then it overwrites along a key.

352
00:21:55,861 --> 00:21:59,548
At the far end, it reflects and rotates, and can track structure.

353
00:22:00,866 --> 00:22:01,664
To recap.

354
00:22:01,984 --> 00:22:06,785
Every rule is S t equals S t minus 1 times A t, plus W t.

355
00:22:07,105 --> 00:22:11,868
The transition decides what the state can do: nothing, fade, fade per channel,

356
00:22:11,868 --> 00:22:13,699
overwrite, or track structure.

357
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:19,698
Left over: the transition changes how the table is edited, never how big it is.

358
00:22:20,018 --> 00:22:24,546
Next, a second way to read all of these rules: the state as a small model,

359
00:22:24,546 --> 00:22:25,954
trained while it reads.

360
00:22:27,267 --> 00:22:30,917
Chapter five: the state as a small model, trained while reading.

361
00:22:31,237 --> 00:22:33,624
So far we have treated the state as a table.

362
00:22:33,944 --> 00:22:36,323
There is a second way to read all of these rules.

363
00:22:36,643 --> 00:22:39,477
Think of S as the weights of a small linear model,

364
00:22:39,477 --> 00:22:41,461
one that should map keys to values.

365
00:22:41,781 --> 00:22:45,596
Then each write is one gradient step, on a loss for the newest pair.

366
00:22:46,700 --> 00:22:47,442
Picture it.

367
00:22:47,762 --> 00:22:50,423
A key goes in, the model S makes a prediction,

368
00:22:50,423 --> 00:22:53,490
and the loss compares that prediction with the value.

369
00:22:53,810 --> 00:22:57,237
The gradient tells S how to change, and one step is taken.

370
00:22:57,557 --> 00:22:59,104
Then the next token arrives.

371
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:03,967
Linear attention uses the loss minus the inner product of S k and v.

372
00:23:04,287 --> 00:23:06,920
Its gradient is minus v k transpose,

373
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:11,819
so one step adds v k transpose: exactly the linear attention write.

374
00:23:12,139 --> 00:23:17,133
DeltaNet uses the squared error, one half the norm of S k minus v, squared.

375
00:23:17,453 --> 00:23:21,847
Its gradient is the error times k transpose, which gives the delta rule.

376
00:23:22,134 --> 00:23:25,869
RetNet and Mamba-2 add a scalar decay to the first loss,

377
00:23:25,869 --> 00:23:28,605
and Gated DeltaNet adds it to the second.

378
00:23:28,925 --> 00:23:32,531
So linear attention and DeltaNet differ only in the loss,

379
00:23:32,531 --> 00:23:34,745
and the decay acts as weight decay.

380
00:23:35,034 --> 00:23:38,422
A toy, with d equal to 3 and beta equal to 1.

381
00:23:38,742 --> 00:23:41,973
The state maps e 1 to e 1, and e 2 to e 2.

382
00:23:42,293 --> 00:23:45,303
A new pair arrives: e 1 should now map to e 3.

383
00:23:45,623 --> 00:23:48,642
The linear attention step reads back 1, 0, 1.

384
00:23:48,962 --> 00:23:51,504
It keeps the old value, with an error of 1.

385
00:23:51,824 --> 00:23:56,638
The delta step reads back exactly 0, 0, 1, with an error of zero.

386
00:23:56,934 --> 00:24:01,306
This view suggests the next moves: a richer model, a better optimizer,

387
00:24:01,306 --> 00:24:02,368
or a better loss.

388
00:24:03,667 --> 00:24:10,109
TTT layers, from 2024, make the state a model, f, either linear or a small MLP,

389
00:24:10,109 --> 00:24:14,023
and train it on each token with a gradient step.

390
00:24:14,343 --> 00:24:19,957
The key, the value and the query are learned projections of the input: theta K x,

391
00:24:19,957 --> 00:24:21,689
theta V x, and theta Q x.

392
00:24:22,800 --> 00:24:28,589
Updating after every token is slow, so TTT updates in mini batches of b tokens.

393
00:24:28,909 --> 00:24:33,219
All the gradients in a batch use the weights from the start of the batch,

394
00:24:33,219 --> 00:24:35,640
so b gradients run as one matrix product.

395
00:24:35,934 --> 00:24:40,025
With b equal to 1, this is online learning, close to the delta rule.

396
00:24:40,345 --> 00:24:44,860
With b equal to the whole sequence, a linear f, a step size of one half,

397
00:24:44,860 --> 00:24:48,309
and zero start weights, it is exactly linear attention.

398
00:24:48,629 --> 00:24:51,286
TTT uses b equal to 16.

399
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:55,996
In the reported 125 million parameter ablation,

400
00:24:55,996 --> 00:25:01,421
perplexity falls from 15.91 for linear attention to 11.09.

401
00:25:01,741 --> 00:25:06,434
The mini batch step alone is the largest single gain, minus 1.70.

402
00:25:08,134 --> 00:25:14,122
Titans, from 2024, adds two optimizer ideas to the memory update: momentum,

403
00:25:14,122 --> 00:25:15,320
and forgetting.

404
00:25:16,434 --> 00:25:17,519
First, surprise.

405
00:25:17,839 --> 00:25:21,267
The gradient is large when the memory's prediction is wrong,

406
00:25:21,267 --> 00:25:23,667
so surprising tokens get written strongly.

407
00:25:23,987 --> 00:25:28,686
Second, momentum. m t keeps writing for a few tokens after a surprise,

408
00:25:28,686 --> 00:25:32,378
so the context around a surprising token is stored too.

409
00:25:32,667 --> 00:25:33,718
Third, forgetting.

410
00:25:34,038 --> 00:25:35,855
Alpha t acts as weight decay.

411
00:25:36,175 --> 00:25:40,967
In the reported ablation, removing it costs the most,

412
00:25:40,967 --> 00:25:44,493
raising perplexity from 27.01 to 29.04.

413
00:25:44,800 --> 00:25:46,740
The memory is a deep MLP.

414
00:25:47,060 --> 00:25:51,015
The price is that a deep memory loses the neat chunk algebra,

415
00:25:51,015 --> 00:25:54,321
so it trains slower than Mamba-2 or Gated DeltaNet.

416
00:25:55,634 --> 00:25:57,928
The delta rule fits only the newest pair.

417
00:25:58,248 --> 00:26:00,824
Two 2025 methods fit more.

418
00:26:01,144 --> 00:26:06,922
ATLAS optimizes the memory on a window of the last c pairs, with the Muon optimizer,

419
00:26:06,922 --> 00:26:12,012
and with polynomial features that raise the capacity bound from order d k,

420
00:26:12,012 --> 00:26:13,938
to order d k to the power p.

421
00:26:14,234 --> 00:26:19,286
Mesa solves the least squares problem over all pairs, exactly, at read time.

422
00:26:19,606 --> 00:26:24,695
The output is G t, times the inverse of H t plus lambda, times q t.

423
00:26:25,015 --> 00:26:29,169
Here G sums v k transpose, and H sums k k transpose.

424
00:26:30,267 --> 00:26:38,419
Take two correlated keys. k 1 equals 1, 0, and maps to plus 1. k 2 equals 0.6, 0.8,

425
00:26:38,419 --> 00:26:40,383
and maps to minus 1.

426
00:26:40,703 --> 00:26:47,328
Reading at k 1 gives 0.40 for linear attention, 0.04 for DeltaNet,

427
00:26:47,328 --> 00:26:49,134
and 1.00 for Mesa.

428
00:26:49,454 --> 00:26:50,780
The price is compute.

429
00:26:51,100 --> 00:26:55,661
With k conjugate gradient steps, Mesa costs about k times GLA.

430
00:26:57,367 --> 00:27:00,202
Small mini batches leave the GPU idle.

431
00:27:00,522 --> 00:27:03,948
With b equal to 16 and a width of 64,

432
00:27:03,948 --> 00:27:08,576
a TTT update reaches only about 11 FLOPs per byte,

433
00:27:08,576 --> 00:27:12,742
far below the roughly 290 that an H100 needs.

434
00:27:13,034 --> 00:27:18,535
LaCT, from 2025, updates only once per 2 thousand to 1 million tokens,

435
00:27:18,535 --> 00:27:21,600
so each update is a big matrix product.

436
00:27:21,920 --> 00:27:27,865
It reports up to 70 percent GPU use, against under 5 percent for earlier TTT,

437
00:27:27,865 --> 00:27:30,491
and its state can grow far larger.

438
00:27:30,800 --> 00:27:33,266
Because a chunk never sees its own update,

439
00:27:33,266 --> 00:27:37,787
every LaCT layer is paired with sliding window attention, for the local part.

440
00:27:39,500 --> 00:27:41,140
Here is the family at a glance.

441
00:27:41,460 --> 00:27:44,855
Each layer picks a loss, an optimizer, and a kind of state.

442
00:27:46,167 --> 00:27:46,965
To recap.

443
00:27:47,285 --> 00:27:50,258
Every write is one gradient step on the newest pair.

444
00:27:50,578 --> 00:27:56,120
TTT, Titans, ATLAS, Mesa and LaCT change the model, the optimizer, or the loss.

445
00:27:57,034 --> 00:27:59,174
Left over: learning is not recall.

446
00:27:59,494 --> 00:28:02,023
A better learner still writes into a fixed state.

447
00:28:02,343 --> 00:28:03,482
Next: the limits.

448
00:28:04,800 --> 00:28:06,268
Chapter six: limits.

449
00:28:06,588 --> 00:28:08,029
Learning is not recall.

450
00:28:08,349 --> 00:28:12,050
Every mechanism in this series improves how the table is written.

451
00:28:12,370 --> 00:28:17,786
But a fixed state still holds only about d k exact pairs, per d v by d k state.

452
00:28:18,106 --> 00:28:19,705
A KV cache keeps all N.

453
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:21,207
Remember chapter one.

454
00:28:21,527 --> 00:28:26,407
A space with d k dimensions has room for only d k orthogonal keys.

455
00:28:26,727 --> 00:28:30,979
Every later mechanism kept the table cleaner, but none of them made it larger.

456
00:28:31,267 --> 00:28:33,541
Here is a small simulation of that limit.

457
00:28:33,861 --> 00:28:35,340
The state is 8 by 8.

458
00:28:35,660 --> 00:28:39,485
We store random key value pairs, then read every stored key back.

459
00:28:39,805 --> 00:28:43,514
With plain additive writes, the reads blur as soon as keys overlap.

460
00:28:43,834 --> 00:28:47,459
With the best possible write, an exact least squares solve,

461
00:28:47,459 --> 00:28:49,547
every read is exact up to 8 pairs.

462
00:28:49,867 --> 00:28:53,111
Past 8 pairs, both errors climb, and they keep climbing.

463
00:28:54,900 --> 00:28:57,183
This is the limit no write rule escapes.

464
00:28:57,503 --> 00:29:01,756
A better write keeps the reads clean up to d k pairs, but past d k,

465
00:29:01,756 --> 00:29:03,851
every fixed state starts to blur.

466
00:29:04,167 --> 00:29:06,407
The reported numbers show the same gap.

467
00:29:06,727 --> 00:29:12,647
In one comparison of 1 billion parameter models on in context recall tasks,

468
00:29:12,647 --> 00:29:16,357
the best fixed state layer, Mesa, scores 41.79.

469
00:29:16,677 --> 00:29:19,489
A Transformer scores 52.27.

470
00:29:19,800 --> 00:29:24,173
Going from plain additive writes to an exact solve gains about 4 points.

471
00:29:24,493 --> 00:29:27,160
Keeping the full cache gains 10.5 more.

472
00:29:29,867 --> 00:29:33,998
Log linear attention, from 2025, is a middle ground.

473
00:29:34,318 --> 00:29:38,739
Instead of one state, it keeps one state per power of two bucket of the past,

474
00:29:38,739 --> 00:29:39,887
like a Fenwick tree.

475
00:29:40,207 --> 00:29:44,554
The output adds up the read of each bucket's state, with a weight lambda for each.

476
00:29:45,667 --> 00:29:50,339
Recent tokens get small buckets, and far tokens are merged into big ones.

477
00:29:50,659 --> 00:29:52,740
Watch the buckets as tokens arrive.

478
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:59,873
At token 13, the buckets are tokens 0 to 7, 8 to 11, and 12, plus the token itself.

479
00:30:00,193 --> 00:30:01,375
That is 4 states.

480
00:30:02,683 --> 00:30:05,696
Each bucket is itself a linear attention state,

481
00:30:05,696 --> 00:30:08,324
the sum of v k transpose over its tokens.

482
00:30:08,644 --> 00:30:13,741
When a new token arrives, two buckets of the same size merge into one twice as big,

483
00:30:13,741 --> 00:30:15,215
like carrying in binary.

484
00:30:15,517 --> 00:30:23,579
After about a million tokens, two to the 20, a KV cache holds 1,048,576 records.

485
00:30:23,899 --> 00:30:27,055
Log linear attention holds at most 22 states.

486
00:30:27,375 --> 00:30:28,991
Linear attention holds 1.

487
00:30:30,283 --> 00:30:33,541
So the number of states grows with the logarithm of the length.

488
00:30:33,861 --> 00:30:36,780
Double the context, and you add just one more state.

489
00:30:37,083 --> 00:30:41,755
The reported gains in perplexity are small, and the gap to Transformers remains.

490
00:30:43,450 --> 00:30:47,893
So in practice, models keep a few full attention layers for exact recall,

491
00:30:47,893 --> 00:30:50,144
and make the rest fixed state layers.

492
00:30:50,464 --> 00:30:53,633
That is the hybrid design, in Part 5 of the essay.

493
00:30:53,950 --> 00:30:57,073
Tasks that need one exact detail from far back,

494
00:30:57,073 --> 00:31:00,063
like a number to repeat or a passage to copy,

495
00:31:00,063 --> 00:31:02,654
still need attention over real records.

496
00:31:02,974 --> 00:31:05,143
The many cheap state layers carry the rest.

497
00:31:06,850 --> 00:31:08,616
Let's put the whole series together.

498
00:31:08,936 --> 00:31:12,629
Chapter one: linear attention turned the cache into one matrix,

499
00:31:12,629 --> 00:31:15,678
written with outer products and read with the query.

500
00:31:15,983 --> 00:31:19,427
Chapter two: decay let the state forget, at a fixed rate,

501
00:31:19,427 --> 00:31:23,172
at a rate chosen by the input, or at one rate per key channel.

502
00:31:23,483 --> 00:31:27,885
Chapter three: the delta rule read the old value and wrote only the error,

503
00:31:27,885 --> 00:31:30,086
so a single entry could be corrected.

504
00:31:30,383 --> 00:31:36,101
Chapter four: all of these became one rule, S t equals S t minus 1 times A t,

505
00:31:36,101 --> 00:31:39,293
plus W t, differing only in the transition.

506
00:31:40,383 --> 00:31:43,567
Chapter five: each write became one gradient step,

507
00:31:43,567 --> 00:31:48,469
which opened the door to richer models, better optimizers, and better losses.

508
00:31:49,783 --> 00:31:50,581
To recap.

509
00:31:50,901 --> 00:31:53,822
A fixed state can be written better and better,

510
00:31:53,822 --> 00:31:57,987
but it holds only about d k exact pairs, while a cache holds all N.

511
00:31:58,883 --> 00:32:02,083
Left over: exact recall still needs real records.

512
00:32:02,403 --> 00:32:07,029
That is why hybrids mix a few full attention layers with many fixed state layers.

513
00:32:07,349 --> 00:32:08,505
Thanks for watching.
